<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1.jpeg"><img decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="438" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-126015" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1.jpeg 1388w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1-300x157.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1-1024x535.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1-768x401.jpeg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>One thing that&#8217;s annoyed me for yonks is how slow Emacs is when displaying images.  Emacs has never been fast on this point, exactly, and has gotten better over the years &#8212; but at the same time, image sizes have exploded.  In the bad old days, images were just a couple of megabytes, but with modern cameras, images can be 25MB, easily.</p>
<p>So now it&#8217;s really painful to page through directories of images:</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/posterwcayUz001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-mFr749.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>It takes half a second for each image, and if you don&#8217;t find that annoying, you&#8217;re way more patient than I am.</p>
<p>Those are from my camera, and I&#8217;ve adjusted the image size down to &#8220;just&#8221; 10MB &#8212; because it&#8217;s just too painful to deal with 25MB images all around.  For instance, when posting a 25MB file to WordPress, it uses about half a minute to scale it down to various sizes.  Not fun.</p>
<p>The Emacs slowness in this area has, in the past, led me to use other non-Emacs ways of looking through images.  So horrible!  When you find images you want to copy an image somewhere else, for instance, you have to go through all these non-ergonomic contortions&#8230;  It&#8217;s a complete nightmare!  Have you people seen non-Emacs interfaces!?  They&#8217;re awful!</p>
<p>Now, Emacs has things like <tt>image-dired</tt> that will go through a directory and create thumbnails, which makes things more liveable.  But it does that by creating thumbnails up-front, which I just don&#8217;t like.  And it doesn&#8217;t really help with the display of the images themselves when you want to look at them.</p>
<p>Now, what&#8217;s the baseline here?</p>
<pre style="white-space: preserve nowrap;">larsi@corrigan:/home/sony/ftp$ time djpeg DSC09468.JPG >/dev/null

real	0m0.128s</pre>
<p>Well, just decoding one of these 10MB images takes more than a tenth of a second.  Emacs also resizes images before display, so this is perhaps a more fair comparison:</p>
<pre style="white-space: preserve nowrap;">larsi@corrigan:/home/sony/ftp$ time convert -resize 1500x DSC09468.JPG jpg:- >/dev/null

real	0m0.264s</pre>
<p>So &#8212; large images just take a lot of time anyway, and there&#8217;s not much we can do about that.</p>
<p>But what if we cheated?</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/poster6NCbb6001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-aNRUNp.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>Whoa.  No, those li&#8217;l images haven&#8217;t been resized and cached (like <tt>image-dired</tt> does) &#8212; they&#8217;re extracted directly from the exif metadata: All modern cameras store a thumbnail inside the images.  So I&#8217;m just extracting them and displaying them on-the-fly.  As you can see from the screencast there, it&#8217;s so fast that even if I jump randomly around in the buffer, it keeps up.</p>
<p>(It inserts thumbnails for the lines displayed in the window, and then the next and a half window&#8217;s worth, thinking that you&#8217;re presumably going to scroll down.  And then whenever you move around, it inserts the thumbnails for the page you&#8217;re currently viewing.  So we&#8217;re not wasting preciouses electricity on things that are never going to be shown &#8212; my test directory here has 8K images.)</p>
<p>If the images don&#8217;t contain thumbnails, the new mode does things the hard way &#8212; by calling <tt>convert</tt> to scale the images down.  This is, of course, a lot slower, but things run asynchronously, anyway, so the UX is UXing, as they say.</p>
<p>(What&#8217;s that?  They don&#8217;t?  They don&#8217;t say that?  They should!)</p>
<p>But what about looking at the pictures in full, then?  That&#8217;s as slow as before, surely?</p>
<p>Let&#8217;s have a look at what&#8217;s in the JPEG:</p>
<pre style="white-space: preserve nowrap;">larsi@corrigan:/home/sony/ftp$ exiftool -json -a -G1 -s -preview:all DSC09471.JPG
[{
  "SourceFile": "DSC09471.JPG",
  "IFD1:ThumbnailImage": "(Binary data 7453 bytes, use -b option to extract)",
  "MPImage2:PreviewImage": "(Binary data 287215 bytes, use -b option to extract)"
}]</pre>
<p>There&#8217;s a 1616&#215;1080 &#8220;preview&#8221; image embedded in there, too!</p>
<p>(Side note &#8212; amusing JSON format here, with the size of the image (in bytes) embedded in a string.  Which also includes helpful command line options.  Such JSON.)</p>
<p>So:</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/poster4SHOdB001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-YamJJ3.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>Emacs displays these previews so fast without doing any magic that it refreshes faster than I can hit <tt>n</tt>.</p>
<p>If there are no thumbnails, the mode precomputes the next dozen images, so you only see the slowness if you never take any pauses.  Lay off that <tt>n</tt> finger, man!  Not cool!</p>
<p>(Or rather, it will do that tomorrow &#8212; my fingers got too tired from typing all day long&#8230;)</p>
<p>In addition, the new mode also allows looking at entire hierarchies with <tt>M-x image-triage-tree</tt>, which is useful when doing image archaeology.</p>
<p>The useful Dired commands are present in this mode, too &#8212; you can mark images (both from the listing view and the per-image buffer), and then copy them over somewhere.  (This was the main reason I wrote this &#8212; I had a bunch of old images I wanted to go over and copy over the useful ones (therefore the name <tt>image-triage</tt>).)</p>
<pre style="white-space: preserve nowrap;">(defvar-keymap image-triage-list-mode-map
  "RET" #'image-triage-display-image
  "SPC" #'image-triage-list-toggle-mark
  "m" #'image-triage-list-mark
  "U" #'image-triage-list-unmark-all
  "u" #'image-triage-list-unmark
  "g" #'image-triage-list-reload
  "v" #'image-triage-list-view-in-dired
  "C" #'image-triage-list-copy
  "D" #'image-triage-list-delete
  "s" #'image-triage-list-toggle-sort)</pre>
<p>It&#8217;s not feature complete yet, I guess&#8230;  but seems to work fine for my use case, at least.</p>
<p>And I wrote the mode by hand!  So there!  Except that I got Claude to write a helper command (I&#8217;ve written exif parsers by hand twice already, so I didn&#8217;t want to do it again) in C to dump thumbnails, because <tt>exiftool</tt> is just too slow:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="224" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-126010" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23.jpeg 1535w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-300x80.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-1024x274.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-23-768x205.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>So the new C programme uses half a millisecond to extract the thumbnail.  <tt>exiftool</tt> uses&#8230;  er&#8230;  let&#8217;s see&#8230;  carry the one&#8230;  0.072s to do it.  Is that right?  Yeah, I guess &#8212; <tt>exiftool</tt> is a Perl script, after all.  So the C program is&#8230;  144x faster.  <img src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/17.0.2/72x72/1fae0.png" alt="🫠" class="wp-smiley" style="height: 1em; max-height: 1em;" /></p>
<p>Now you&#8217;re asking yourself &#8212; hasn&#8217;t somebody already written a thumbnail extractor that you can just <tt>apt install</tt> instead?  Yes, <tt>exiv2</tt> exists and is fast&#8230;  but can only extract thumbnails, and not previews.  And!  It can&#8217;t extract to <tt>stdout</tt> &#8212; it insists on writing to the same directory as the image is in, so it&#8217;s a pain to deal with.  So whatchagonnado.</p>
<p>(Typical Linux situation &#8212; one solution that&#8217;s general but slow, and one solution that&#8217;s fast but outdated and awkward to use.)</p>
<p>But I understand many people are opposed to using LLMs, so you don&#8217;t need to compile the extra binary.  In that case, things look like this:</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/posteralODKW001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-2qClPY.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>Not as impressive, but I guess it&#8217;s usable &#8212; it&#8217;s asynchronous, after all.</p>
<p>So there you go.</p>
<p>I&#8217;ve put the code on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/image-triage.el" data-cached-time="2026-11-23T19:50:19" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cache-2026-11-23-web.webp">Microsoft Github</a>, so you can give it a whirl if you want.</p>
